id: pm-sf31yl
title: "The bounded read costs 13.8 times the unbounded read to deliver the same set, because eight metadata blocks are re-emitted per page and the page carries two rows"
description: "Measured at HEAD on the live tracker. pm list --json returns all 315 open items in 71,709 bytes, about 228 bytes per row. pm list --for triage returns 2 rows in 6,250 bytes, about 3,125 bytes per row, with has_more true and a resumable cursor. Walking the same 315 items through the triage intent therefore takes 158 pages and 987,500 bytes, which is 13.8 times the single unprojected call and about 247,000 tokens against about 18,000. The token-efficiency feature is anti-efficient at whole-set scope, and it is anti-efficient by construction rather than by accident. Every page re-emits eight metadata blocks that do not vary across the chain: context_intent, row_contract, projection, omission_receipt, filters, sorting, completeness and the pagination triple. On a 2-row page those blocks and the wider per-row field set dominate the payload, so the marginal cost of a row is small compared to the fixed cost of asking for it. Two independent levers exist and neither is applied. The metadata is chain-invariant, so it can be emitted once for the cursor chain and referenced by fingerprint on continuation pages, or suppressed by an explicit caller opt-out. The row count is chosen without reference to measured per-row cost, so a budget that admits 1,045 tokens of payload is spent mostly on envelope instead of being divided by the observed bytes-per-row to pick the largest feasible page. This is scoped to per-page metadata amortization and page-size derivation across the paginated read family. It is not the per-row resume cursor in pm events, which is pm-ez1dfg, and it is not the encoding study, which is pm-kvxqp1."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: context-management,pagination,token-budget
created_at: "2026-07-30T17:01:49.124Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-01T05:31:16.734Z"
