comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-01T00:37:53.206Z","harness:codex","Delivery evidence 2026-08-01: continuation pages now replace invariant filtering, sorting, projection, completeness, row, omission, pagination-summary, timestamp, and full intent-receipt blocks with a cursor-fingerprint reference while retaining only rows and the next cursor. List page size is budget-derived and the live tracker delivers 20 rows at 1703/3200 tokens; cursor integrity tests traverse list/search/context without duplicates. Remaining acceptance before closure: add a mandatory full-walk cost gate on a generated current-scale corpus, its repeated-metadata negative control, and publish the before/after whole-chain table."
  "2026-08-01T01:34:20.523Z","harness:codex","PR #849 review reconciliation: CodeRabbit asked that the full-walk gate, repeated-metadata negative control, and before/after chain table be linked before closure. The item is intentionally still open, its existing delivery comment explicitly names those three remaining artifacts, and it has not been claimed or closed in this review pass. No unsupported artifact links were fabricated."
  "2026-08-01T01:45:28.919Z","harness:codex","Review-fix evidence: post-projection row reduction is now restricted to next:execute, whose contract has a scalar primary recommendation; list/get/search/context instead fall back to receipt-only output rather than silently dropping paginated or otherwise retrievable rows. Added a no-cursor list regression and a 2,000-row serialization-efficiency regression. Focused review tests and the complete sandboxed coverage suite pass at 6637/6637 with 100% statements, branches, functions, and lines. Remaining full-walk current-scale cost-gate and before/after chain artifacts stay open and are not claimed as delivered."
  "2026-08-01T02:15:21.645Z","harness:codex","Exact-head CodeRabbit cleanup: removed two non-discriminating absence assertions from the row-compaction regression; the test now asserts only compaction behavior driven by its fixture. Focused context/MCP regressions and ESLint pass. Remaining full-walk acceptance remains open."
  "2026-08-01T02:15:24.601Z","harness:codex","Exact-head CodeRabbit cleanup: removed two non-discriminating absence assertions from the row-compaction regression; the test now asserts only compaction behavior driven by its fixture. Focused context/MCP regressions pass 18/18. Remaining full-walk acceptance remains open."
  "2026-08-01T05:27:14.209Z","harness:codex","Cursor-chain evidence 2026-08-01: full ordered list/search walks match their unbounded baselines exactly with no duplicate, omission, or reorder. List walks 1,998 rows in 30 pages, 344,234 optimized bytes versus 1,558,741 unbounded bytes (0.2208); search walks 1,998 rows in 64 pages, 402,075 bytes versus 1,681,847 (0.2391). Repeating first-page invariant metadata is measurably more expensive (379,875 and 454,869 bytes). Coverage testing additionally found and fixed stale-cursor risk by restoring rows before fail-closed omission when a compacted row lacks an ID."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-30T17:21:52.247Z","harness:claude-code","Measurement discipline this issue depends on, worth stating because the intuitive measurement hides the defect: judge a bounded read by the cost of the whole answer, never by the cost of one page. Per page the triage projection looks like a clear win at 6,250 bytes against 71,709 for the unprojected call. Multiplied by the pages needed to reach the same 315 rows it is 987,500 bytes, and the win becomes a 13.8-fold loss. The comparison that matters is total tokens to answer the question the caller actually asked.\n\nThe derived quantity to record for any paginated surface is bytes per row at the offered page size, because it exposes envelope amortisation directly: 228 bytes per row unprojected against 3,125 per row at a two-row page is the whole finding in one ratio, and it stays meaningful across corpus sizes in a way that a page byte count does not."
